# The Path of Art!
Tamil title: கலைவழி!

The field of art is growing prosperously today. I say “prosperously” rather than “excellently” with a depth of meaning. I do not prevent anyone who wishes to regard prosperous growth itself as excellence!

The people's relationship with art continues to grow. In gait, clothing and manner, I see the “colour” of art settling upon people, whether they recognise it or not.

From the art of beauty to the art of the kitchen, we see novelty and abundance glittering. All that glitters is certainly not gold; but neither can we rule that lying dim and dull is itself the mark of dignity.

Thinking, speaking, studying and writing about art are increasing. There are countless newspapers, journals, special issues and pamphlets. Every journal has made a place for art, and many periodicals devoted specifically to art are published. These journals help people think about art, while the journals benefit from the people's interest in art. A healthy relationship is developing.

Some say art elevates the condition of society; others say that art is lowering it. From what both sides say, it becomes evident that art has the power to change society's condition. That itself gives some pleasure, for does it not show that those who lament that art has lost its power and that its former benefits can no longer be seen are not speaking the truth? Art remains powerful. Its radiance is growing, its connection with the people is rapidly expanding, and it is prosperous.

When art possesses power, radiance, popular connection and prosperity, it is not good to neglect the field, abandon the direction and character of its growth to chance, or fail to take responsibility for what must be obtained through it.

There is little need to worry about whether a barren woman knows how to fondle a child. But must we not discover whether a mother has the ability to protect and raise the treasure she has borne?

Art is not barren. To speak in the language of cinema, art is a “fortunate queen who has borne children.” We must therefore not remain indifferent, saying, “Let whatever comes through art come, and let whoever receives it receive it.” Once we know that art is a queen capable of bearing children, arrangements must exist for that queen to cherish and praise them and enable them to grow with distinction.

Nallathangal too was a “fortunate queen who bore children,” but, alas, she was forced to throw them into a well and kill them.

Likewise, once we recognise that art has the fertility to give birth to many kinds of “benefit,” we must not allow its course to remain unattended.

Art is powerful. But is that power directed towards destruction or creation?

Art is radiant; no one denies it. But does that radiance kindle cravings and attachments in the heart, or make purity flourish?

Art has a close relationship with the people. But should that relationship be like teacher and student, police officer and accused, or comrades? What form should the relationship take?

Art is prosperous. But should that prosperity increase every form of the people's well-being, their body, mind, material condition and culture? Or is it enough merely to enrich those engaged in the field?

Many such questions arise.

Do you say that no one raises questions of this kind? The frequent quarrels among those engaged in art, and the feelings of difference between them and people in other fields, are all consequences of these “questions” swinging in their minds. What else could they be?

Because they do not approach these questions with the aim of ordering them, refining them and gaining clarity, and because such an approach is difficult, those in the world of art take an easier path and shoot arrows of ridicule, abuse, blame and curses at one another.

Problems that arise naturally turn into quarrels.

No one in the arts can escape these problems, and the remedy is not for them to trade insults with one another.

Art possesses power and radiance. Its connection with the people continues to grow, and its prosperity shines. True! Along what path should this opportunity travel?

“What sort of question is that? If you ask which path it should take, it should take the good path,” you will reply. People do answer this way, easily and confidently. I see some sweeten their answer with syrup and others mix harshness into it.

When such an easy answer exists, you may wish to ask why I pretend there is something difficult in the question. I see the difficulty not embedded in the question asked, but in the answer received.

When asked which path it should take, they say, “the good path.” I am very pleased.

But which is the good path?

Who is competent to prescribe it?

Does the good path suit only our own time, or does it endure for ever?

How are we to accept that the person declaring “this is the good path” is truly qualified to do so?

These are only some of the questions for which you and I must think repeatedly and seek answers.

When a doctor says, “This is malaria; I have prescribed quinine,” no one becomes doubtful and asks, “How does this person know? How can we accept what he says as correct?” People say yes, and they pay him.

Likewise, the opinions of people engaged in particular specialised fields are accepted by others. Few deny, doubt or dispute them.

But in answer to “What is the good path?” we cannot and should not categorically declare that certain people are qualified to answer and that everyone else has a duty to listen and consent. Nor is there any need to do so.

That is why an answer to “What is the good path?” does not conclude the case; it opens the way to new disputes.

What, then, is the solution?

There is one. Neither accepting without thought what others identify as the good path, nor rejecting it and multiplying hostility through argument, will produce true benefit.

Victory sometimes brings down the very people who won it, and the strange situation may arise in which the plaintiff becomes the defendant.

The meaning of “good path” must therefore be determined not merely from what others say or from our own isolated reflection. We must first reach a conclusion about where we wish to go and where we firmly believe our society ought to go. The best road to that destination must then be regarded as the good path.

This involves greater difficulty, but once skill in this method is acquired, it also brings sweetness.

To determine properly where we should go, we must know where we are today and understand the evils that presently exist.

Is it not the person who has stood in the sun who knows the value of shade?

Where do we and our society stand today?

We stand amid caste and the divisions it produces; superstition and the destruction it ignites; economic differences and the hatred that boils up from them; disorder in government and the frenzied passions and repulsive acts it arouses; lack of self-confidence; evasion of responsibility; and weariness, all mixed together.

If one points to a red lotus growing in mud, the flower gains distinction, but the mud itself does not become excellent. Likewise, pointing to clear-minded individuals found amid so many degrading qualities, or displaying their speeches, does not prove that society is in a fine condition.

We must travel to a “good place” from which these degrading qualities have been removed, where they have disappeared and noble qualities abound.

The road to that destination is the good path!

What use is boarding a bus to Kanchipuram while intending to reach Kasi?

How can one reach Papanasam by boarding a train to Bombay merely because it gleams brightly?

So it is with our journey. Once we establish the destination, the road that carries us there is, for us, the good road.

Develop maturity in this method; then the path will become clear and the journey easy.

When reading a book, delighting in a song, rejoicing at a dance, becoming enthralled by a film, feeling stirred by a painting, experiencing great pleasure from a speech, or finding bliss in beauty, this one purpose must serve as measuring rod, balance and touchstone. Those who create these artistic experiences need it in full measure too.

If art is attended to in this manner, it will yield prosperity. The field will not merely remain prosperous; it will shine with excellence.

(*Dravida Nadu*, Pongal special issue, 14-1-1;28)

---NOTES---
The OCR title `கலைவமி/` is corrected in the display title to the clearly recoverable `கலைவழி!`; the verbatim source remains unchanged. The article distinguishes `செழிப்பு` (material or abundant prosperity) from `சிறப்பு` (excellence or distinction). Nallathangal is the tragic mother of Tamil legend who kills her children by casting them into a well. The closing year `1;28` is damaged OCR and requires scan verification; it is retained exactly rather than guessed.
